§§ `Assertions -- Chapter 18, aggravated forgery, forged documents, false instruments and accounts`

-- The base: Illustration (a) to s 464 is s 463 forgery, and needs the dishonest fault.
#ASSERT `constitutes forgery` `altering a letter of credit` `dishonest fault`
#ASSERT NOT (`constitutes forgery` `altering a letter of credit` `blank fault`)
-- ss 466 to 469 each add one thing to that forgery; a plain forgery is none of them.
#ASSERT `constitutes forgery of a valuable security or will` `altering a letter of credit` `dishonest fault` `a forged will`
#ASSERT NOT (`constitutes forgery of a court record or public register` `altering a letter of credit` `dishonest fault` `a forged will`)
#ASSERT `constitutes forgery of a court record or public register` `altering a letter of credit` `dishonest fault` `a forged court record for cheating`
#ASSERT `constitutes forgery for the purpose of cheating` `altering a letter of credit` `dishonest fault` `a forged court record for cheating`
#ASSERT NOT (`constitutes forgery to harm reputation` `altering a letter of credit` `dishonest fault` `a forged court record for cheating`)
#ASSERT NOT (`constitutes forgery of a valuable security or will` `altering a letter of credit` `dishonest fault` `blank forged document`)
-- and without the underlying forgery, the description alone is nothing.
#ASSERT NOT (`constitutes forgery of a valuable security or will` `altering a letter of credit` `blank fault` `a forged will`)
-- s 471 takes its fault from ss 24 and 25.
#ASSERT `constitutes using a forged document as genuine` `using a forged certificate` `dishonest fault`
#ASSERT NOT (`constitutes using a forged document as genuine` `using a forged certificate` `blank fault`)
-- s 474 needs a s 466 or s 467 description.
#ASSERT `constitutes possession of a forged document` `holding a forged will to use it`
#ASSERT NOT (`constitutes possession of a forged document` `holding a forged private letter`)
-- ss 472 and 473 split on which forgery the seal is for.
#ASSERT `constitutes making or possessing a counterfeit seal for a section 467 forgery` `a counterfeit seal for a will`
#ASSERT NOT (`constitutes making or possessing a counterfeit seal for another forgery` `a counterfeit seal for a will`)
-- s 473A is complete on knowledge; s 473B needs the two-part intent and prejudice.
#ASSERT `constitutes possessing equipment for making a false instrument` `a card printer known to make false cards`
#ASSERT NOT (`constitutes possessing equipment for making a false instrument to induce prejudice` `a card printer known to make false cards`)
#ASSERT `constitutes possessing equipment for making a false instrument to induce prejudice` `a card printer to defraud a bank`
#ASSERT NOT (`constitutes possessing equipment for making a false instrument` `a card printer to defraud a bank`)
-- s 473C(2): an act the person has an enforceable duty to do is disregarded.
#ASSERT NOT (`constitutes possessing equipment for making a false instrument to induce prejudice` `a card printer to induce a payment already owed`)
#ASSERT NOT (`the act or omission intended to be induced is to a person prejudice` `a card printer to induce a payment already owed`)
-- s 473C(3) and (4): a machine induced to respond is prejudice.
#ASSERT `the act or omission intended to be induced is to a person prejudice` `a false card to fool a machine`
-- ss 475 and 476 split on whether the mark authenticates a s 467 document.
#ASSERT `constitutes counterfeiting a mark for a section 467 document` `a counterfeit stamp for a share certificate`
#ASSERT NOT (`constitutes counterfeiting a mark for another document` `a counterfeit stamp for a share certificate`)
#ASSERT `constitutes counterfeiting a mark for another document` `a counterfeit stamp for a licence`
#ASSERT NOT (`constitutes counterfeiting a mark for a section 467 document` `a counterfeit stamp for a licence`)
-- s 477: the intent-to-injure route needs no s 24 or s 25 fault; the document must be a will, adoption authority or valuable security.
#ASSERT `constitutes fraudulent cancellation of a will or valuable security` `burning a rival will` `blank fault`
#ASSERT NOT (`constitutes fraudulent cancellation of a will or valuable security` `burning an ordinary letter` `blank fault`)
-- s 477A is confined to clerks, officers and servants.
#ASSERT `constitutes falsification of accounts` `a clerk cooking the books`
#ASSERT NOT (`constitutes falsification of accounts` `an outsider cooking the books`)

§§ `Assertions -- Chapter 18, currency and bank notes`

-- ss 489A to 489C: making, passing and holding counterfeit notes.
#ASSERT `constitutes forging or counterfeiting currency` `printing counterfeit notes`
#ASSERT `constitutes using or trafficking in counterfeit currency` `passing a counterfeit note knowingly`
#ASSERT NOT (`constitutes using or trafficking in counterfeit currency` `passing a counterfeit note unknowingly`)
#ASSERT `constitutes possession of counterfeit currency` `holding counterfeit notes to spend`
#ASSERT NOT (`constitutes possession of counterfeit currency` `passing a counterfeit note knowingly`)
-- s 489D needs no counterfeit note to exist.
#ASSERT `constitutes possessing instruments for counterfeiting currency` `owning a note-printing plate`
#ASSERT NOT (`constitutes forging or counterfeiting currency` `owning a note-printing plate`)
-- s 489E conjoins the s 107 abetment conduct.
#ASSERT `constitutes abetting the counterfeiting of currency abroad` `abetting a counterfeiting ring abroad` `instigation where a different act was done`
#ASSERT NOT (`constitutes abetting the counterfeiting of currency abroad` `abetting a counterfeiting ring abroad` `blank abetment`)
-- s 489F takes its fault from ss 24 and 25; s 489G has its own intention.
#ASSERT `constitutes diminishing or adulterating a coin` `clipping a gold coin` `dishonest fault`
#ASSERT NOT (`constitutes diminishing or adulterating a coin` `clipping a gold coin` `blank fault`)
#ASSERT `constitutes altering the appearance of currency` `repainting a two dollar note as a fifty`
#ASSERT NOT (`constitutes diminishing or adulterating a coin` `repainting a two dollar note as a fifty` `dishonest fault`)
-- ss 489H and 489I: passing and holding altered currency.
#ASSERT `constitutes delivering altered currency` `passing a repainted note`
#ASSERT NOT (`constitutes possession of altered currency` `passing a repainted note`)
#ASSERT `constitutes possession of altered currency` `holding repainted notes to spend`
#ASSERT NOT (`constitutes delivering altered currency` `holding repainted notes to spend`)

§§ `Abetment fixtures -- sections 109, 110, 112 and 115 to 120`
-- s 109 Illustration (a): A offers a bribe to B, a public servant, who accepts it. The s 161 offence is committed in consequence.
`instigating a bribe that is accepted` MEANS Abetment WITH
    `instigates any person to do the thing` IS TRUE
    `engages with one or more other persons in a conspiracy for the doing of the thing` IS FALSE
    `an act or illegal omission takes place in pursuance of that conspiracy and in order to the doing of that thing` IS FALSE
    `intentionally aids by any act or illegal omission the doing of the thing` IS FALSE
    `the thing would be an offence if committed by a person capable of committing an offence with the same intention or knowledge as the abettor` IS TRUE
    `the abetment takes place in Singapore` IS FALSE
    `the act abetted would be committed outside Singapore` IS FALSE
    `the act abetted would constitute an offence if committed in Singapore` IS FALSE
    `the offence abetted would be committed in Singapore` IS FALSE
    `any or all of the acts constituting the abetment were done outside Singapore` IS FALSE
    `facts unknown to the abettor make the doing of the thing impossible` IS FALSE
    `a different act from the act abetted was done` IS FALSE
    `the act done was a probable consequence of the abetment` IS FALSE
    `the act done was committed under the influence of the instigation or with the aid or in pursuance of the conspiracy which constituted the abetment` IS FALSE
    `the act was abetted with the intention on the part of the abettor of causing a particular effect` IS FALSE
    `an act for which the abettor is liable in consequence of the abetment causes a different effect from that intended` IS FALSE
    `the abettor knew that the act abetted was likely to cause that effect` IS FALSE
    `the abettor was present when the act or offence for which he would be punishable was committed` IS FALSE
    `the act abetted is committed in consequence of the abetment` IS TRUE
    `express provision is made by this Code or by any other written law for the punishment of the abetment` IS FALSE
    `the person abetted does the act with a different intention or knowledge from that of the abettor` IS FALSE
    `the act for which the abettor is liable under section 111 is committed in addition to the act abetted and constitutes a distinct offence` IS FALSE
    `the offence abetted is punishable with death or imprisonment for life` IS FALSE
    `the offence abetted is punishable with imprisonment` IS FALSE
    `abets the commission of an offence by the public generally or by any number or class of persons exceeding 10` IS FALSE
    `intends to facilitate or knows it to be likely that he will thereby facilitate the commission of an offence` IS FALSE
    `voluntarily conceals by any act or illegal omission the existence of a design to commit that offence or makes any representation which he knows to be false respecting that design` IS FALSE
    `the offence designed is punishable with death or imprisonment for life` IS FALSE
    `the offence designed is punishable with imprisonment` IS FALSE
    `is a public servant whose duty as such it is to prevent the commission of the offence designed` IS FALSE
    `the offence designed is committed` IS FALSE
